 "findings": [
  "Live ESPN scoreboard fetches succeeded on all 164 queried league-days (failed_days=[] and rejected={} in the job log); 1,224 rows published, manifest hashes match, zero duplicate event_ids, zero malformed rows.",
  "Only 2 of 1,224 rows are SCORE_UNRECONCILED (NHL; period sums differ from ESPN final by one goal) - kept, flagged, excluded from distributions by default.",
  "Coverage is the CURRENT season only: NFL from 2026-09-09, CFB from 2026-08-29, NBA from 2026-10-20, NHL from 2026-10-06, CBB a single day (2026-11-02, season opener). No prior seasons were collected, so per-period distributions rest on n=107 (NBA), 121 (NFL), 182 (CBB, one slate), 192 (NHL), 622 (CFB) games.",
  "Every row is POST-GAME. observed_at for ALL rows is the single backfill time (2026-11-03T17:56-17:57Z), not when the result first became public: an observed_at-gated as-of join would exclude every historical row from any backtest before 2026-11-03. Historical rows therefore need availability = game end (not recorded) when used as lagged features; this is a limitation, not a fix.",
  "The published data branch is an orphan branch force-pushed by the backfill workflow, so append-only holds INSIDE a run (existing event_ids are skipped) but not across runs: a rerun replaces the branch. Rerun idempotency of the collector is covered by unit test only; ESPN is unreachable from the authoring sandbox so no live rerun was done here.",
  "Collector defect fixed in this PR: the per-request throttle was dead code (the sleep was skipped whenever a fetcher existed, which is always true after defaulting), so the backfill issued ~165 requests unthrottled. It now sleeps only for real network fetches.",
  "Not stored: ESPN overtime/shootout resolution (only n_periods>regulation), so NHL shootout record and OT-period scoring are not derivable from these rows."
 ],
